IMO, implicit string concatenation is a bug, not a feature.<p>I once made a stupid mistake of having a list of directories to delete:<p><pre><code>    directories_to_delete = (
        &quot;&#x2F;some&#x2F;dir&quot;
        &quot;&#x2F;some&#x2F;other&#x2F;dir&quot;
    )
    for dir in directories_to_delete:
        shutil.rmtree(dir)
</code></pre>
Can you spot the error?  I somehow forgot the comma in the list.  That meant that rather than creating a tuple of directories, I created a single string.  So when the `for` loop ran, it iterated on individual characters of the string.  What was the first character?  &quot;&#x2F;&quot; of course.<p>I essentially did an `rm -rf &#x2F;` because of the implicit concatenation.

